  "cScaleSummary": "C2 because tuna is the primary cholesterol-contributing ingredient, with the amount spread across multiple servings. The noodles, mushrooms, onion, celery, green beans, olive oil, flour, stock, skim milk, mustard, lemon juice, parsley, breadcrumbs, and seasonings contribute little to no cholesterol.",
  "ldlActiveSummary": "Not LDL-Active because the recipe is centered on tuna, an animal protein. It remains a lower-cholesterol casserole because the creamy body comes from mushrooms, stock, skim milk, flour, and technique rather than butter, cream, cheese, egg, or condensed soup, but it does not qualify as actively LDL-lowering under the EWHF standard.",
  "yield": 6,
  "yieldUnit": "servings",
  "tags": [],
  "intro": "Tuna noodle casserole is one of the great American pantry casseroles: practical, creamy, familiar, and built for a table that wants comfort more than fuss. The classic midcentury version leaned on canned soup and dairy, but the essential idea is better than that shortcut. Tender noodles, tuna, mushrooms, green beans, and a crisp top can still deliver the old-school casserole feeling when the sauce is built carefully from browned mushrooms, stock, and a light thickener instead of heavy cream.",
